  "tldr": "Watson et al.'s sharp \\(p=2\\) leaky-IV interval is a square root of an observable quadratic feasibility slack. Every covariance-based sample object is totalized by one measurable \\(n^{-2}\\) ridge rule, whose near-singular event is uniformly asymptotically negligible. At equality, the positive-part plug-in endpoints have a fourth-root Gaussian law, whereas the package's feasibility-retained bootstrap converges to a random truncated kernel. A one-dimensional multiplier band is inverted before the square-root map. The resulting hull is intended to cover the whole identified interval uniformly, attain \\(n^{-1/4}\\) expected outer directed-Hausdorff loss at contact and \\(n^{-1/2}\\) loss in the fixed interior under an explicit empty-set loss convention, and meet a LAN lower bound on a fully verified positive-definite fixed-relevance Gaussian path.",
